  "about": "Development cases, second set. Written by a separate agent session as the planner changes that followed the first comparison began, labelled with the behaviour the product should have, without running any planner. The session's hand-back included the cases, so the engineer changing the planner saw them partway through the work: they are scored as development cases, not held out. Every company is one of the 28 in the recorded runtime's fixture universe snapshot. Categories: periods (recency word, number as digits or words, quarters, years or months), company_forms (possessives, a leading 'The', '&' versus 'and', short names, $TICKER, trailing punctuation, 'vs', 'against', 'stack up'), follow_up ('add', 'include', 'also', 'too' extend the companies; 'what about', 'how about', 'same for' replace them, keeping the metric and window), common_word_names (Apple, Oracle and Intel used as a company versus as an ordinary word), ranking, refusal (unknown metric or advice) and clarify (ambiguous metric words, ADR 0004). Period rules labelled against: 'N years' is 4N quarters, 'the past year' is 4, 'a few' or 'several' quarters is 4, 'a couple of' is 2, '18 months' is 6. No two of the 28 are different companies sharing a name (GOOG and GOOGL are one company), so there is no ambiguous-company case. Expectations use the same fields and scoring as planner-cases.json and apply to the last turn.",
